WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.450
So we've now seen that the algorithm that
we have, this branch

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:07.400
and bound algorithm, is not going to
perform well on noisy data sets.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:11.640
And as soon as we go from 18 amino
acid masses

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:16.886
to 144, we're going to start to really
struggle with using this algorithm.

00:00:16.886 --> 00:00:19.020
We're not going to give up on it, we just
need to

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:23.030
figure out if we can find more
information that's in that spectrum.

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:25.360
Is there something that the spectrum is
telling us that we just haven't

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:26.040
been listening to?

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:30.110
So our goal here is to say, we've got to

00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:32.900
get the number of amino acid masses that
we consider down.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:34.536
We've got to reduce these.

00:00:34.536 --> 00:00:37.580
So, we've got to go from 144, wouldn't it
be nice

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:40.220
to knock that down to 50, or 20 or even
10,

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:41.060
in a great scenario?

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:47.450
Let's recall this spectrum that we had,
this hypothetical experimental spectrum.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:50.020
And let's just see what we notice about
it.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:50.840
This is a great

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:56.060
problem solving strategy, where
if you're stuck on a solution,

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:59.970
one of the most common things to do
is: consider a small example.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.060
Look and see what that small example
is

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:05.510
telling you, and see if that maybe
generalizes.

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:06.630
So here's our small example.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:08.590
It's just going to be for a peptide of
length four.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:13.010
Then we're going to say, okay, well, we
can't detect

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:16.440
the mass of E, it's not in this
experimental spectrum,

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.840
so we're not going to identify that E is

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.630
one of our letters, one of our amino acids
that way.

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:24.460
But…hold on.

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:27.940
We do detect 257 which is the mass of QE.

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:32.670
And we do detect 128 which is the mass of
Q.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:33.890
 

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:38.300
If you subtract these two masses, you
should get the mass of E.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:39.050
 

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:42.070
E is what's left over with QE and Q.
So when you subtract

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:42.867
them, you get 129.

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:47.090
So that's good.
Can we find it anywhere else?

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:49.110
The answer is yes.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:55.320
ELN is another subpeptide of this cyclic
peptide NQEL, as is LN.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:00.540
And we notice that the masses of these two
are in the spectrum as well.

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:03.840
We get 356 minus 227 and that gives us
129.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.089
We even get one more.

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:09.630
The mass of the entire peptide is 484.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:14.660
And the mass of LNQ, the subpetide that
doesn't include E, is 355.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:19.990
And when we subtract those we get 129.
So 129 is still there, right?

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:23.470
It may not be on the spectrum, but if we
start subtracting masses

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:27.138
that are in the spectrum, then we're
going to get three occurrences of 129.

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:31.290
This is going to motivate our definition
of what's called a “spectral convolution”.

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:32.820
So the convolution of a spectrum.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:38.260
It's the positive difference between
every pair of masses in the spectrum.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:40.450
So here is the same spectrum that we had,

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:43.890
we represent the masses along here,
and along here,

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.850
and we go through and we subtract every pair.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:50.480
We have 227 here as the mass of LN.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:53.270
We don't know it's the mass of LN, but we
have 227.

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:57.320
And here we have the mass detected of 370
and when we subtract

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:58.180
them, we get 143.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:01.140
And we can do this for every pair and we
form this table.

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:06.830
Now, I've highlighted certain masses, and
I'll tell you why I've done that.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:08.900
The reason is, we want to ask, what are

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:11.780
the most frequent elements between 57 and
200?

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:14.420
Because remember with E, we picked it up
in a

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:16.730
number of different places, we picked it
up three different times.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:19.580
So we want to ask ourselves, what are the

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:22.460
most frequent elements between 57 and 200,
and there's

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:25.540
a good chance that's going to give us what the
amino acids should be.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:30.560
The five most frequent elements that I
highlighted

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.920
are: 99, 113 114, 128 and 129, and when

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:39.350
we convert those from the integer mass
table, we see that that's V, L, N, Q, and E.

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:39.660
 

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:41.390
They correspond to those letters.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:44.510
If we take the five most frequent
elements,

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:46.660
four of them are going to give us the amino acids
of NQEL.

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:50.530
This is really promising on this small example.

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:53.790
So now we have the

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:56.820
outline of an algorithm called
ConvolutionCyclopeptideSequencing,

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:58.950
and the first thing that it's going to do
is to say

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:04.440
OK, given our experimental spectrum,
let's form the spectral convolution of it.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:06.350
Then we're going to look at that spectral
convolution and

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:08.730
say, “what are the most frequent elements
in it”?

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:10.910
We may choose this parameter,
capital M,

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:15.020
taking the M most frequent elements
between 57 and 200,

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:17.390
so M may be 50 or 20 in practice.

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:22.870
And then we're going to run our
leaderboard algorithm using only those

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:28.060
top M masses that we found between 57 and 200
in the spectral convolution,

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:30.799
and we're going to form our peptides only
on those integer masses.

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:36.950
The question then is,
does this algorithm really work?

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:39.210
That's the question that we constantly
ask, right?

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:40.870
And so let's consider Spectrum10.

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:43.884
Remember, when we expanded our alphabet,

00:04:43.884 --> 00:04:46.960
the leaderboard method didn't work for
Spectrum10.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:49.390
So, let's see if it works for Spectrum10

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:51.400
now that we're dealing with the
convolution.

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:53.509
So the first step is,
take the convolution.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:56.980
Then we pick the ten most frequent
elements in

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:59.110
that convolution, which I'm showing here
on the right.

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:07.500
Most of these are actually standard masses
or correspond to standard amino acids.

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:11.610
So 145 was nonstandard but the other ones
were identified as standard.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:14.242
Then we run the algorithm on only these

00:05:14.242 --> 00:05:16.410
amino acids,so these are the only amino
acids that

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:20.150
will be allowed in our peptide, and the
winning peptide

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:22.910
is Tyrocidine B1 and we should be
happy.

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:25.440
So it reconstructed the correct peptide
just

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:27.510
from taking the ten most frequent elements

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:29.569
in the spectral convolution, so it worked
really, really well.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:35.010
Well then we ask ourselves, OK, what
about Spectrum25?

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:37.350
What about this noisier spectrum that
none of our

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:42.400
algorithms has been able to correctly
reconstruct Tyrocidine B1 from?

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:43.450
What happens if we apply

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:47.830
ConvolutionCyclopeptideSequencing
to that really noisy spectrum?

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:49.450
And when we do it, it's good news.

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:53.250
We can reconstruct Tyrocidine B1 again and
so now we can start the party.

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:57.080
But I do want to mention one more thing

00:05:57.080 --> 00:05:59.670
about practical considerations, and I'll do that
in the next section.

